# ARCHIVED 2026-11-25 (Mike): dropped from Lesson 3 as too advanced (avoidance play: duck the first spade to cut East's communication). Save for a later lesson.
# Lesson 3, new exercise: dummy's clubs are missing the ace and king. Lose both early, while you still hold stoppers.
title: Two honors to force out
id: L03-15
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2, 3.4, 3.5
student: S
board: 47
dealer: S
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A72    Q82    54     QJT98
  E  53     J976   QJ9    AK76
  S  K64    AK5    AK72   432
  W  QJT98  T43    T863   5

auction:
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = 8-9 points, balanced: invites game
  E Pass
  S 3NT*   = Accepts: a maximum 1NT
     key: 17 is the top of your range. Accept the invitation.
     not Pass: Partner invited you to bid on with the top of your range. Did you notice that 17 is the top?
     not *: Partner's 2NT asks: are you at the top of your 15-17?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner invited with 2NT. With 17, the top of your
  range, you accepted. You are declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and
  so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 10
  spades: 2 = The ace and king. One in each hand, to stop the spades twice.
  hearts: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  diamonds: 2 = The ace and king.
  clubs: 3 = Three, once the ace and king are gone.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Dummy's clubs are missing the ace and the king. How many tricks will they give once both are gone?
play:
  1   W SQ  N S2  E S3  S S4*
      note: Seven winners on top: two spades, three hearts and two diamonds. Dummy's clubs give three more, but only after the ace and the king are gone, so you'll lose the lead twice. Duck the first spade: let West's queen win.
      S4 key: Duck. You'll have to lose two club tricks anyway, so lose a spade now, while you can afford it.
      S4 not SK: Did you think about the two club tricks you must lose? If you duck now, East may have no spade left to lead back when he wins a club.
  2   W SJ  N S7  E S5  S SK
      note: Now win with your king. Dummy's ace stays as a second stopper, and a way back to the clubs.
  3   S C2  W C5  N CQ  E CA
      note: Start on clubs right away.
  4   E DQ  S DA  W D3  N D4
      note: East has no spades left to lead, so he tries diamonds. You still have the ♦A.
  5   S C3  W D6  N CJ  E CK
      note: Keep going: force out the king.
  6   E HJ  S H5  W H3  N HQ
      note: East still has no spades. Dummy's ♥Q wins, and you're in dummy with the clubs.
  7   N CT  E C6  S C4  W H4
      note: Dummy's clubs are all winners now.
  8   N C9  E C7  S D2  W S8
      note: Keep cashing dummy's clubs.
  9   N SA  E H6  S S6  W S9
      note: Dummy's ♠A.
  10  N C8  E H7  S D7  W D8
      note: The last club.
  11  N H8  E H9  S HK  W HT
      note: Over to your ♥K...
  12  S HA  W ST  N H2  E D9
      note: ...and your other winners.
  13  S DK  W DT  N D5  E DJ
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: count seven winners on top, then force out the ♣A and ♣K to set up
  dummy's clubs.

  You had to lose the lead twice in clubs, so you lost a spade early too.
  Ducking the first spade took away East's spades. Here East held both club
  honors, so it didn't matter. But if West had held one of them, East could
  have won the first club and led a spade back, and West would have set up
  his spades with the other club honor still to come as a way back in.

  Lose the tricks you have to lose early in the play.
